{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat decisions/D042-external-research-probes-1.md decisions/D041-qwen-moe-cache-screen-and-ncu.md decisions/D004-profiler-qualification.md decisions/D011-bandwidth-and-q8-profile.md; cat artifacts/bpf1/bpf.txt; head -12 artifacts/ncu1/ncu.csv; rg --files artifacts/codexPROFW cc | head -60'","aggregated_output":"# D042 — External-research probes, round 1 (INDICATIVE): n-gram speculation, perf, bpftrace, research intake\n\nStatus: INDICATIVE. These are single-round screens, no protocol was frozen, and the host was quiet. Date: 2026-11-10. Engine: release-1 (`sha256:5b8a6a22…`).\nResearch intake: `research/intake-1.json` and `research/INTAKE.md`, written by a Sonnet agent.\n- Channels that worked: paper-gatherer, `gh`, and the HF API.\n- Semantic Scholar rate-limited abstract lookups (HTTP 429), and the GitHub MCP failed to connect.\n\n## 1. N-gram self-speculation (`llama-server --spec-type`), Qwen3-30B-A3B Q4_K_M, K=30, op T-SPEC1/1b\nSettings: 256 tokens, temperature 0, single slot. Scripts: `artifacts/spec1/probe*.py`.\n\n| Prompt | base t/s | ngram-mod t/s | ngram-map-k t/s | drafts accepted | Text identical |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---|---|\n| code rewrite (`/no_think`) | 35.6 | **85.2 (2.4×)** | 60.2 | 225/225 | yes |\n| list repeat (`/no_think`) | 35.2 | **106.6 (3.0×)** | 36.4 | 229/229 | yes |\n| open chat | 36.9 | 36.7 | 34.8 | 0 drafts | yes |\n\n- **Engagement lesson.** Run T-SPEC1 used prompts without `/no_think`. Qwen3 then began with free-form reasoning, so no drafts were made, and the probe looked like \"no effect\".\n  - Always log `timings.draft_n` and `draft_n_accepted`. Without them, the absence of a gain can't be told apart from speculation never running.\n- **Result: lossless, and gated by workload.** The factory should enable `ngram-mod` for copy-heavy work (editing, RAG quoting, code refactors). It costs nothing on chat.\n- **Why it suits offloaded MoE.** Verifying a batch of drafted tokens reads the CPU-resident experts once per batch instead of once per token. That cuts the dominant per-token CPU byte cost (see §3).\n- The Granite all-GPU repetitive prompt reached 1787 t/s, about 2.2× its baseline.\n\n## 2. perf (host change: `perf_event_paranoid` 4→1, runtime only; system-wide counting runs through `sudo perf`)\n- Zen 2 on kernel 6.8 exposes **no `amd_df` / UMC PMU**, so true DRAM bandwidth can't be counted.\n- The proxy, L1 data-cache fills from DRAM × 64 B, gave 14.9 GB/s during Qwen all-CPU-expert decode (24.4 t/s).\n  - It undercounts because it misses fills made only by the L2 prefetcher.\n  - IPC was 0.58 across 16 threads.\n- Conclusion: **CPU bandwidth must come from a microbenchmark** (T-CAL), not counters. perf remains useful for IPC and cycles.\n\n## 3. bpftrace uprobes on the CPU backend (sudo; target `/proc/<container pid>/root/app/libggml-cpu.so`)\nSettings: Qwen K=48, all experts on the CPU, 5 s window. Output: `artifacts/bpf1/bpf.txt`.\n- 5626 calls to `ggml_graph_compute`, which is **48.9 per token at 23.1 t/s**. That is one CPU split per layer.\n- CPU split time was **4.45 s of 5 s (89% of wall time)**, with a median per-split time of 0.5–1 ms (mean 0.79 ms).\n  - This independently confirms D039's 0.757 ms per CPU layer.\n  - The remaining 11% is GPU work, handoff and sampling.\n- **Pitfalls:**\n  - Use `docker inspect -f '{{.State.Pid}}'`. `pgrep` matches the host docker client.\n  - The CPU MoE matmul function is a templated static (`ggml_compute_forward_mul_mat_id_tiled_one_expert<…>`). Probe the C-exported `ggml_compute_forward_mul_mat_id_tiled` or `ggml_graph_compute` instead.\n- This is a zero-rebuild, low-overhead per-layer timer, and it should become a CC (`bpf_split_timer`).\n\n## 4. Intake priorities (see intake-1.json)\n1. Rebase onto upstream moe-cache changes after the pin. PR #30966 CPU-share targets our 4 GB loss in D041.\n2. Rebase onto ClassLRU, #31233 and #31853.\n3. Hit-rate regression issue #31421: check the hit rate at batch 1.\n4. MTP with Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-MTP GGUF (about 21 GB download).\n5. REAP-pruned Qwen3-30B (15B).\n6. S2-MoE (arXiv 2608.15018, llama.cpp-based code).\n7. Speculation and expert-bytes model.\n8. CUDA fusion PRs.\n\n## 5. Host calibration (T-CAL) first real run — NEGATIVE, fixed in T-CAL2\n- The CPU probe first failed with rc 127. The engine binary links `libcuda` even with `-b CPU`, so every engine-image probe needs `--gpus`. That is fixed in calibrate.py.\n- Constants after the rerun:\n  - GPU: 1384 GB/s. **Impossible**; the peak is about 896 GB/s.\n  - CPU: Q4_K 97, Q8_0 147 GB/s. **Impossible**; the DDR4 peak is about 50 GB/s.\n  - H2D: 28.7 GB/s. Plausible for PCIe 4.0.\n  - Handoff: 199 µs. Plausible; PL4's fitted value was 137 µs.\n- Cause: test-backend-ops perf reuses one weight buffer, about 33 MB on the GPU and about 7 MB on the CPU. Those fit the 48 MB GPU L2 and 32 MB CPU L3.\n- **Methodology rule:** every bandwidth probe must (a) use a working set of at least 8× the last-level cache and (b) pass a physical-ceiling plausibility guard. T-CAL2 adds both.\n# D041 — Upstream `--moe-cache-mib` beats static offload on Qwen at 8 GB (INDICATIVE); ncu decode roofline\n\nStatus: **INDICATIVE**. The screen was unfrozen, with 2 alternating rounds and no pre-registered protocol. A frozen follow-up is required before the factory adopts this.\nDate: 2026-11-10. Lane: x86-main.\nEngine: release-1 (`run_ref sha256:5b8a6a22…`, pin 31288c9c6). That pin contains upstream PR #29887, `--moe-cache-mib`.\nScript: `artifacts/mc-qwen/screen.sh`, op T-MCQ. Raw output is in `artifacts/mc-qwen/`.\n\n## Why\nD022 found that static `--n-cpu-moe` beats the engine LRU cache at every footprint. That test used **Granite**, whose experts are 0.84 MiB each.\nExternal reports for the upstream cache show decode gains on 16–32 GB cards with large MoEs (R9700 11→27.6 t/s; 5060 Ti 16 GB 15→19 t/s). They also say a hit rate of about 75% is needed.\nD031 had already flagged a proxy-scale bias, so the D022 conclusion needed retesting on Qwen3-30B-A3B Q4_K_M.\n\n## Result\nSettings: chat prompt, 128 tokens, greedy decoding, FA on, ctx 4096. Peak VRAM is from nvidia-smi polled every 0.5 s.\n\n| Config | Peak MiB | Decode t/s (r1, r2) | Prompt eval ms | Text sha |\n|---|---:|---|---:|---|\n| static `--n-cpu-moe 30` | 8193 | 36.88, 37.33 | 757, 783 | 1189257b |\n| all-CPU experts + `--moe-cache-mib 6000` | 7831 | **51.13, 51.44** | 1082, 1089 | bf80f280 |\n| static `--n-cpu-moe 42` | 4105 | 27.93, 26.77 | 1000, 996 | 77a3045a |\n| all-CPU experts + `--moe-cache-mib 2200` | 4029 | 18.00, 18.15 | 1088, 1088 | bf80f280 |\n\n- **At about 8 GB, the cache gives +38% decode with 362 MiB less VRAM.**\n  - TTFT is about 40% worse, 1085 ms against 770 ms. This matches the reported prompt-processing loss.\n- At about 4 GB, the cache is −34%. The crossover lies between the two budgets, and the hit rate is the likely driver.\n  - That fits T-QRT's coverage curves. Held-out decode coverage is 77% at the K=8-sized hot set, falling to 15% at K=42.\n- Every placement produced different greedy text, including static K30 against K42. This is the usual numeric divergence between CPU and GPU, not evidence of a bug. Even so, it needs a **KLD/PPL check**, not text identity.\n  - The two cache configs produce identical text, which is consistent with all expert math running on the GPU.\n- The host was quiet: no compiles, load average 2.4.\n\n## Consequences\n1. The D022 scope is narrowed to \"small experts / low coverage\". The planner must not hard-reject the expert cache.\n2. Planner: add the cache as a lever. Model the hit rate from routing-trace coverage curves (T-QRT), and price misses at PCIe upload cost. The decision rule becomes: cache when predicted coverage ≥ about 70%, otherwise static.\n3. The report must show the TTFT cost. The factory should expose a \"decode-optimised vs TTFT-optimised\" choice.\n4. Frozen follow-up: ABBA ×3 at budgets 4/6/8/10/12 GB, with the cache size swept to find the crossover. Add KLD against a reference and the hit rate from engine logs.\n\n## ncu decode roofline (Granite Q8_0, release-1, all-GPU), op T-NCU1\nSettings: `ncu --launch-skip 2000 --launch-count 600` on `llama-bench tg32`. Raw data is in `artifacts/ncu1/ncu.csv`.\nCaveat: ncu serialises kernels and flushes caches, so small-kernel times are inflated against graph replay.\n\n- The effective DRAM bandwidth over the profiled window is 222 GB/s. The 5070 Ti's peak is about 896 GB/s.\n- The large expert `mul_mat_vec_q` reaches 578 GB/s (66% of peak DRAM).\n- About 40% of kernel time goes to tiny 2–4 µs kernels at under 1% DRAM: `quantize_q8_1`, `rms_norm`, `add`, `scale`, `topk_moe`, `moe_weighted_reduction`.\n  - On a model this small, decode is launch- and latency-bound, not bandwidth-bound.\n  - **Fusion candidates:** rms_norm with quantize_q8_1, and scale with add.\n- An unprofiled nsys timeline is needed to size the real gain. ncu's serialisation exaggerates small kernels.\n# D004 — Profiler qualification (host RmProfilingAdminOnly=1, perf_event_paranoid=4; GPU RTX 5070 Ti sm_120)\nImage: campaign/cuda-tools:c1 (FROM nvidia/cuda:12.8.1-devel-ubuntu24.04 + cmake, git, python3, cuda-nsight-systems-12-8 = nsys 2024.6.2; ncu 2025.1.1). Build: builds/cuda-tools/Dockerfile.\nEvidence: artifacts/nsight-qual/, ops in evidence/orchestrator.jsonl (descs \"nvcc build vecadd\", \"ncu WITHOUT...\", \"ncu WITH...\", \"nsys ...\").\n| Capability | Result |\n|---|---|\n| nvcc -arch=sm_120 build + run on GPU | works |\n| ncu, no extra caps | FAILS ERR_NVGPUCTRPERM |\n| ncu, container --cap-add SYS_ADMIN | works (SpeedOfLight section OK) — narrowest workable, no host change |\n| nsys GPU/CUDA/NVTX/OS-runtime trace, no caps | works (cuda_gpu_kern_sum stats OK) |\n| nsys CPU sampling / ctx-switch | NOT qualified (perf_event_paranoid=4); run with --sample=none --cpuctxsw=none; default invocation did generate a report but CPU sampling coverage unverified |\nHost-wide NVreg change (approved by user) therefore NOT applied/needed so far. No reboot or driver reload.\nObservation: first kernel launch 23.8 ms vs ~28 us steady-state (managed-memory page faults) -> profiles of short runs must discard/flag warmup.\nLimit: this qualifies tools on a toy kernel only; MoE kernels, replay overhead, and report size for the real engine are still unmeasured.\n# D011 — Bandwidth screen (ncu) and Q8_0 decode profile; engine candidate selected\nEvidence: artifacts/profiles/tg_matmul_ncu.{ncu-rep,csv} (ncu 2025.1.1, SYS_ADMIN, profiling-only timings, NOT inference speed); q8_tg128_{node,graph}.{nsys-rep,sqlite}.\nBF16 (D008): MoE expert matvec (grid 512x8, 16.8 MB, 25 us) = 75% DRAM peak (~665 GB/s, spec peak 896 GB/s is a published value, not measured here); attention projections (1-2 MB, 4-6 us) only 30-44% => latency-bound; mul_mat_f 63%. => BF16 kernel-level headroom small (<~7% overall); weight bytes are the lever (confirmed by Q8_0 +32% decode, D009).\nQ8_0 decode: graph exec median 1171 us + gap 178 us => 741 tok/s (profiled graph-level; unprofiled screen 794). 633 kernels/token (vs 487 BF16): +146 quantize_q8_1/token = 14.4% of kernel time, avg 1.6 us each.\nKernel order in a layer shows 3 separate quantize_q8_1 of the same normed activation before the q, k, v matvecs (artifacts/profiles/q8_tg128_node.sqlite).\nSelected engine candidate C1 (H-fuse-q8): reuse one q8_1 quantization of an activation across consecutive consumers. Prediction: remove ~2 quantize launches/layer (~48/token, ~53 us of 1171) => ~3-4.5% decode. Success criterion (frozen before assessment): >= +2% decode on matched counterbalanced A/B (A/A bound 0.5-2%), outputs bit-identical (mean KLD 0, top-1 100% vs unpatched Q8_0), no PPL change. If <2% or non-identical => rejected, reported as such.\nNot chosen now: BF16 kernel tuning (<=7% ceiling), graph-opt env (rejected D008), fusing norm+quantize (larger change, defer).\nAttaching 3 probes...\n\n\n@graph_tot_us: 4451700\n@graph_us: \n[4, 8)                70 |                                                    |\n[8, 16)               45 |                                                    |\n[16, 32)               0 |                                                    |\n[32, 64)               0 |                                                    |\n[64, 128)              0 |                                                    |\n[128, 256)             0 |                                                    |\n[256, 512)             0 |                                                    |\n[512, 1K)           5405 |@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@|\n[1K, 2K)             105 |@                                                   |\n[2K, 4K)               1 |                                                    |\n\n@graphs: 5626\n@s[2484220]: 721614230847234\n==PROF== Connected to process 99 (/w/app/llama-bench)\n==PROF== Disconnected from process 99\n\"ID\",\"Process ID\",\"Process Name\",\"Host Name\",\"Kernel Name\",\"Context\",\"Stream\",\"Block Size\",\"Grid Size\",\"Device\",\"CC\",\"Section Name\",\"Metric Name\",\"Metric Unit\",\"Metric Value\"\n\"0\",\"99\",\"llama-bench\",\"127.0.0.1\",\"quantize_q8_1(const float *, void *, long, long, long, long, long, unsigned int, uint3)\",\"1\",\"14\",\"(256, 1, 1)\",\"(4, 1, 1)\",\"0\",\"12.0\",\"Command line profiler metrics\",\"dram__bytes_read.sum\",\"byte\",\"7936\"\n\"0\",\"99\",\"llama-bench\",\"127.0.0.1\",\"quantize_q8_1(const float *, void *, long, long, long, long, long, unsigned int, uint3)\",\"1\",\"14\",\"(256, 1, 1)\",\"(4, 1, 1)\",\"0\",\"12.0\",\"Command line profiler metrics\",\"dram__throughput.avg.pct_of_peak_sustained_elapsed\",\"%\",\"0.40\"\n\"0\",\"99\",\"llama-bench\",\"127.0.0.1\",\"quantize_q8_1(const float *, void *, long, long, long, long, long, unsigned int, uint3)\",\"1\",\"14\",\"(256, 1, 1)\",\"(4, 1, 1)\",\"0\",\"12.0\",\"Command line profiler metrics\",\"gpu__time_duration.sum\",\"ns\",\"2272\"\n\"0\",\"99\",\"llama-bench\",\"127.0.0.1\",\"quantize_q8_1(const float *, void *, long, long, long, long, long, unsigned int, uint3)\",\"1\",\"14\",\"(256, 1, 1)\",\"(4, 1, 1)\",\"0\",\"12.0\",\"Command line profiler metrics\",\"sm__throughput.avg.pct_of_peak_sustained_elapsed\",\"%\",\"0.30\"\n\"1\",\"99\",\"llama-bench\",\"127.0.0.1\",\"void mul_mat_vec_q<8, 1, 0, 0, 0>(const void *, const void *, const int *, ggml_cuda_mm_fusion_args_device, float *, unsigned int, uint3, unsigned int, unsigned int, unsigned int, uint3, unsigned int, unsigned int, unsigned int, uint3, unsigned int, unsigned int, unsigned int, unsigned int)\",\"1\",\"14\",\"(32, 4, 1)\",\"(1024, 1, 1)\",\"0\",\"12.0\",\"Command line profiler metrics\",\"dram__bytes_read.sum\",\"byte\",\"1121280\"\n\"1\",\"99\",\"llama-bench\",\"127.0.0.1\",\"void mul_mat_vec_q<8, 1, 0, 0, 0>(const void *, const void *, const int *, ggml_cuda_mm_fusion_args_device, float *, unsigned int, uint3, unsigned int, unsigned int, unsigned int, uint3, unsigned int, unsigned int, unsigned int, uint3, unsigned int, unsigned int, unsigned int, unsigned int)\",\"1\",\"14\",\"(32, 4, 1)\",\"(1024, 1, 1)\",\"0\",\"12.0\",\"Command line profiler metrics\",\"dram__throughput.avg.pct_of_peak_sustained_elapsed\",\"%\",\"30.48\"\n\"1\",\"99\",\"llama-bench\",\"127.0.0.1\",\"void mul_mat_vec_q<8, 1, 0, 0, 0>(const void *, const void *, const int *, ggml_cuda_mm_fusion_args_device, float *, unsigned int, uint3, unsigned int, unsigned int, unsigned int, uint3, unsigned int, unsigned int, unsigned int, uint3, unsigned int, unsigned int, unsigned int, unsigned int)\",\"1\",\"14\",\"(32, 4, 1)\",\"(1024, 1, 1)\",\"0\",\"12.0\",\"Command line profiler metrics\",\"gpu__time_duration.sum\",\"ns\",\"4192\"\n\"1\",\"99\",\"llama-bench\",\"127.0.0.1\",\"void mul_mat_vec_q<8, 1, 0, 0, 0>(const void *, const void *, const int *, ggml_cuda_mm_fusion_args_device, float *, unsigned int, uint3,